virglrenderer: clip distances between tessellation shaders as varyings on the Mac (#589)

What: on a desktop GL without cull distances (the Mac's 4.1) a tessellation control shader
that writes clip distances passes them to the evaluation shader as plain per-vertex varyings,
omacvm_clip0[] (and omacvm_clip1[] for more than 4), without a location, instead of
gl_ClipDistance in gl_out[] and gl_in[]. The control shader says so in its variable info
(tess_clip_out, num_out_clip = the distances it stores), the evaluation shader gets it in its
key (tess_clip_in) and then declares the same arrays, also when it reads none, and reads
omacvm_clipK[v][i] (an indirect index picks the array at run time; a distance not passed
reads 0). Not with cull distances, nor when the varyings would not fit (MAX_VARYING): then
both keep gl_PerVertex as before. GLES and hosts with cull distances keep their GLSL.
Why: Apple's GL on Metal builds no pipeline for a draw whose control-to-evaluation interface
holds gl_ClipDistance, on either side ("Fallback to SW vertex processing because
buildPipelineState failed"): the draw ran on Apple's CPU vertex path, Metal's shader compiler
crashed in GLDTessellationControlShader::mergeVSCSToCompute and
GLDTessellationEvaluationShader::parseShaderInput, and the first draw took ~255 ms (M1 Max,
macOS 27). The same values as plain varyings build on the GPU. Apple's software
renderer has no such failure. Apple matches varyings without a location by name (as
virgl-shader-separable-patch-varyings.patch relies on).
Upstream: not sent (a workaround for Apple's GL).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-core-glsl-shaders.c: the GLSL of tessellation shaders with clip
distances on the Mac's config (whole and separable, 1 and 8 distances, an indirect read, an
evaluation shader that reads none) has no gl_ClipDistance in gl_out[] or gl_in[] and compiles
on the Mac's GL; with cull distances or on GLES it keeps gl_PerVertex.
Tests/virgl/test-whole-program-shapes.c C15, C16, C19 and C20 draw the same.

